<section class="box-exercise">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/17:_Alcohols_and_Phenols/17.06:_Reactions_of_Alcohols" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B3.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-36350" id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B3.7D.5C)-36350"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise \(\PageIndex{3}\)</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-36350">Starting with cyclohexanol, describe how you would prepare the following?</p>

<p class="lt-chem-36350">(A) cyclohexyl acetate (B) 1-allylcyclohexan-1-ol (C) cyclohexene (D) ethoxycyclohexane</p>

<dl>
    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Answer</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p class="lt-chem-36350">A) This can be seen as a transesterification, acid and some other ester would be needed to form cyclohexylacetate</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-36350">B) First, oxidize the alcohol to a ketone, take for example Dess-Martin Periodinane, then use an allyl grignard to form 1-allylcyclohexan-1-ol</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-36350">C) Alcohols can dehydrate to form alkenes under acidic conditions, so using anhydrous acid and heat would yield cyclohexene</p>

    <p class="lt-chem-36350">D) The alcohol can also be a nucleophile, perform a halogen substitution, using 1-X ethane, to yield ethoxycyclohexane</p>
    </dd>
</dl>
</div></section>

<section class="box-exercise">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/17:_Alcohols_and_Phenols/17.06:_Reactions_of_Alcohols" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B4.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-36350" id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B4.7D.5C)-36350"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise \(\PageIndex{4}\)</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-36350">In cyclohexanone, a ketone, indicate the polarity of the bond between oxygen and carbon.</p>

<dl>
    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Answer</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36350"><img alt="17_6_3_Answer.svg" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387836/17_6_3_Answer.svg?revision=1" /></p>

    <p class="lt-chem-36350">Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon creating the polar bond. This is the basis for the carbon's electrophilicity.</p>
    </dd>
</dl>
</div></section>

<section class="box-exercise">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(Morsch_et_al.)/17:_Alcohols_and_Phenols/17.06:_Reactions_of_Alcohols" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B5.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-36350" id="Exercise_.5C(.5CPageIndex.7B5.7D.5C)-36350"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise \(\PageIndex{5}\)</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-36350">In the dehydration of 1-methylcyclohexanol, which product is favored?</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-36350"><img alt="17_6_4.svg" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/387837/17_6_4.svg?revision=1" /></p>

<dl>
    <dt><strong class="emphasis bold">Answer</strong></dt>
    <dd>
    <p class="lt-chem-36350">The more substituted alkene is favored, as more substituted alkenes are relatively lower in energy.</p>
    </dd>
</dl>
</div></section>

